{"name":"Barley Corn","hex":"#b6935c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"barley corn","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A grain of barley.","The length of such a grain; a unit of length of approximately one third (or sometimes one quarter) of an inch or eight millimetres, still used as a basis for shoe sizes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barley_corn"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barley","blocks":[{"text":"Barley, a member of the grass family, is a major cereal grain grown in temperate climates globally. One of the first cultivated grains, it was domesticated in the Fertile Crescent around 9000 BC, giving it nonshattering spikelets and making it much easier to harvest. Its use then spread throughout Eurasia by 2000 BC. Barley prefers relatively low temperatures and well-drained soil to grow. It is relatively tolerant of drought and soil salinity, but is less winter-hardy than wheat or rye. Wild barley may refer to H. vulgare ssp. spontaneum, but may also refer to other species in the genus Hordeum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg/330px-Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barley"},{"title":"Barleycorn (unit)","blocks":[{"text":"The barleycorn is an English unit of length equal to 1⁄3 of an inch. It is still used as the basis of shoe sizes in some English-speaking countries."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Barley_grains_3.jpg/330px-Barley_grains_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barleycorn_(unit)"},{"title":"Maize","blocks":[{"text":"Maize, also known as corn, is a tall stout grass that produces cereal grain. The leafy stalk of the plant gives rise to male inflorescences or tassels which produce pollen, and female inflorescences called ears. The ears yield grain, known as kernels or seeds. In modern commercial varieties, these are usually yellow or white; other varieties can be of many colors. Maize was domesticated by indigenous peoples in southern Mexico about 9,000 years ago from wild teosinte. Native Americans planted it alongside beans and squashes in the Three Sisters polyculture."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg/330px-Zea_mays_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-283.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize"},{"title":"Maize (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The shade maize or corn refers to a specific tone of yellow; it is named for the cereal of the same name—maize. In public usage, maize can be applied to a variety of shades, ranging from light yellow to a dark shade that borders on orange, since the color of maize may vary."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Cornheap.jpg/330px-Cornheap.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maize_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Barley Barn at Cressing, Essex, built around 1220; its name means \"barley barley-store\".[3]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarley_Barn_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg/1280px-Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg/500px-Illustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Botanical illustration of leafy stem with roots, flowers, and 2- and 6-row ears","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIllustration_Hordeum_vulgare1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg/500px-Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg/1280px-Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg/500px-Genetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Genetic analysis on the spread of barley from 9,000 to 2,000 BC[16]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGenetic_analysis_on_the_spread_of_barley_from_9000_to_2000_BCE.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Ancient_barley.jpg/500px-Ancient_barley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Ancient_barley.jpg/1280px-Ancient_